\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

\paragraph{Regime Transition Mechanism.}
At $K{=}3$, a majority requires $t{=}2$; a single compromised judge flips the panel, making $\eta_{\max}$ the binding constraint. At $K{\geq}5$, the adversary must compromise ${\geq}3$ judges, and high-$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ panels present more distinct attack surfaces. The transition threshold $K^*$ lies between 3 and 5.

\paragraph{Attack-Mechanism-Dependent Structure.}
The aggregate positive $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ coefficient masks a 6:6 directional split at $K{=}3$ (11/12 consistent at $K{=}5$; Appendix~\ref{app:bft_reversal}). Targeted attacks are consistent with judge-specific vulnerabilities amplified by independence; shared-bias attacks are consistent with common biases diluted by diversity. Unconditional claims about diversity being ``harmful'' or ``beneficial'' are thus misleading.

\paragraph{Diversity--Capability Confound.}
The $r{=}0.70$ correlation between $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\eta_{\max}$ reflects a structural property: maximizing diversity requires sampling across capability tiers, introducing weaker judges. VIF averages 3.0 (10/12 below 5), but observational regression cannot reliably disentangle diversity's causal contribution from the co-occurring capability deficit (Appendix~\ref{app:diversity_capability}). In the full pool, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s independent effect cannot be separated from capability variance in the majority of conditions: Cinelli--Hazlett sensitivity analysis \citep{cinelli2020making} shows that the known $\eta_{\max}$ confound exceeds the robustness value in 28/36 conditions (Appendix~\ref{app:sensitivity}). Robustness increases with $K$ (RV exceeds $\eta_{\max}$ in 1/12 at $K{=}3$, 3/12 at $K{=}5$, 4/12 at $K{=}7$), paralleling the regime transition. The API-only subset ($r{=}0.85$) does not replicate the transition (3/12), raising the possibility that $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ partly proxies for quantization-induced capability variance rather than a pure diversity signal. Capability-matched subsampling ($r{=}0.50$, 9 models, 126 panels at $K{=}5$) provides partial mitigation: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ strengthens to 9/12 and Cinelli--Hazlett RV exceeds the $\eta_{\max}$ confound in 6/12 conditions (conservatively 5/12 excluding near-zero $R^2$; Appendix~\ref{app:sensitivity_poolA}), compared to 3/12 in the full pool. Cross-benchmark prediction further supports this: MMLU-derived $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ predicts ARC vulnerability in 14/18 conditions ($r{\approx}0.76$--$0.78$; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_benchmark}). The transition is thus a descriptive regime shift whose independent causal attribution to diversity requires capability-controlled experimental designs.

\paragraph{Aggregation and Deployment.}
At $K{=}3$, Bayesian calibration reverses $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s direction (6/7 significant coefficients negative) by downweighting weak judges via calibrated confusion matrices (Table~\ref{tab:aggregation}); at $K{=}5$, Bayesian aggregation neutralizes both predictors ($\eta_{\max}$ 1/12, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ 0/12; Table~\ref{tab:aggregation_k5}), eliminating panel composition's predictive power entirely. Robustness-first selection ($\min \eta_{\max}$) achieves lowest ASR in 12/12 conditions at $K{=}3$ (Appendix~\ref{app:panel_selection}). Practitioners should match strategy to panel size: screen for individual robustness at $K{=}3$; prefer correlated robustness profiles at $K{\geq}5$. $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is computed from clean pairwise MI; recomputing MI under attacked conditions confirms stability (Spearman $\rho{\geq}0.91$ for 4/6 attacks), though position bias substantially disrupts MI structure ($\rho{=}0.08$ on ARC), consistent with its anomalous regression behavior (Appendix~\ref{app:attacked_keff}). Fractional logit regression \citep{papke1996econometric} yields 93.1\% directional agreement with OLS and preserves the transition pattern (Appendix~\ref{app:robustness_checks}). The random-fault contrast (\S\ref{sec:rf_contrast}) shows $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ predicts under non-adaptive noise ($R^2 = 0.73$--$0.81$), with the predictability collapse under adaptive attack most pronounced at $K{=}3$ (1/12) and attenuated at $K{\geq}5$ (7/12).

