\title{The Shifting Bottleneck: How Panel Size Modulates\\Adversarial Vulnerability in LLM Judge Panels}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Multi-judge LLM evaluation panels are widely assumed to benefit from judge diversity, drawing on fault-tolerance intuitions from distributed systems. We empirically characterize a panel-size-dependent vulnerability regime transition by decomposing panel attack success into individual judge weakness ($\eta_{\max}$) and structural diversity ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$) across 15 models, six attack types, two benchmarks, and panel sizes $K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$ (up to 6,435 panels). At small panels, individual weakness dominates; at larger panels, diversity structure becomes the primary predictor. This aggregate pattern masks attack-mechanism-dependent structure: higher diversity is associated with increased vulnerability for targeted attacks but decreased vulnerability for shared-bias attacks. Aggregation rules further modulate the relationship, with Bayesian calibration reversing the diversity channel. The defense strategy must match the regime: small panels require screening for individual robustness; larger panels require managing judge independence.
\end{abstract}

